# senbonzakura, in the same sealed box its competitors run in.
#
# WHY OUR OWN TOOL GETS A CONTAINER TOO
#
# The isolation exists to protect the card from somebody else's dependency tree, and by that
# argument our own arms did not need one: we wrote the code, and it runs on the host venv. Running
# them there anyway would have left a difference underneath the table that no column showed.
#
# The host venv carries torch 2.13.0+cu130. The benchmark base pins torch 2.5.1+cu124, because
# that is what the competing tools are pinned to. Two arms on two torch builds are two sets of
# kernels, two sets of numerics, and a KL divergence measured against a slightly different
# baseline. `bench/EQUAL-BUDGET.md` said "Card: same", which was true and which quietly meant
# less than a reader would take it to mean.
#
# So both tools now run on one image, one torch, one CUDA build, with the same flags, the same
# read-only mounts and the same absent network. It also means our own arm is audited by the same
# self-test as everybody else's, which is the answer to a fair question a rival author would ask:
# why is the isolation only applied to other people's code.
#
# WHAT IS BAKED IN, AND WHAT IS NOT
#
# The dependencies are baked; the source is NOT. `bench/run-isolated.sh --senbon-src` mounts the
# working tree read-only at /work/senbon-src, so an arm runs the code that is checked out right
# now and `cli.code_version()` reports it. Baking the source would mean rebuilding the image to
# change one line, and would make the version in the image and the version in the repository two
# things that have to be kept in step by hand.
#
# Build:
#   docker build -f bench/Dockerfile.senbonzakura -t senbon-bench:senbonzakura .
FROM senbon-bench:tool

USER root

# Everything senbonzakura imports, EXCEPT torch, which the base already pins. `--no-deps` is not
# used here: these packages' own dependency trees are wanted, and torch is simply not asked for
# again, so pip has nothing to resolve it against and cannot swap the CUDA build underneath.
#
# Pinned exactly rather than floated, for the same reason the base pins torch: a benchmark whose
# numeric or tokenisation stack moves between arms is not a benchmark. The floors these satisfy
# are in pyproject.toml, with the reason each floor exists. The exact versions are the ones the
# development environment runs, so they are the set the test suite is green against rather than
# whatever a resolver picks on build day.
#
# The torch assertion is the load-bearing line. Any of these packages may pull a different torch
# as a dependency, and it would look like a successful build while silently undoing the whole
# reason this image exists.
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir \
      transformers==5.14.1 \
      accelerate==1.14.0 \
      datasets==5.0.1 \
      optuna==4.9.0 \
 && python -c "import torch; assert torch.__version__.startswith('2.5.1'), torch.__version__" \
 && python -c "import transformers, datasets, optuna, accelerate; print('senbonzakura deps ready')"

USER bench
WORKDIR /work

# The source arrives as a read-only mount rather than a layer, so this is where Python looks for
# it. An arm therefore runs the checkout, not a copy of it made at build time.
ENV PYTHONPATH=/work/senbon-src \
    BENCH_TOOL=senbonzakura

CMD ["python", "-c", "import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/work/senbon-src'); import torch; from senbonzakura import cli; print('senbonzakura', cli.__version__, 'torch', torch.__version__, 'cuda', torch.cuda.is_available())"]
